@@ -0,0 +1,176 @@
+# SPEC: Stock reservations
+
+## Why
+
+Checkout has to hold stock for a cart while payment runs. Then it either turns
+the hold into an order (payment succeeded) or gives the stock back (payment
+failed or was abandoned). If nobody confirms or releases a hold, it must lapse
+on its own so the stock isn't locked forever. There is no background job, so
+the lapse has to happen without anyone calling a sweep. The cart service
+retries requests, so creating a hold must be safe to repeat; an idempotency key
+provides this.
+
+Checkout data: 95% of payments finish within 18 minutes, so a hold lasts
+**20 minutes**.
+
+## Interface (fixed)
+
+`InventoryService(repository, ledger, clock)` keeps working as is.
+
+- `InventoryService.reserve(warehouse: str, items: dict[str, int], idempotency_key: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.confirm_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Order`
+- `InventoryService.release_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.get_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.available(sku, warehouse)` must take holds into account.
+- `inventory.models`: dataclass `Reservation` with `reservation_id: str`,
+  `warehouse: str`, `items: dict[str, int]` (units held per SKU),
+  `status: ReservationStatus`, `expires_at: datetime`, `order_id: str | None`;
+  enum `ReservationStatus` with members `ACTIVE`, `CONFIRMED`, `RELEASED`,
+  `EXPIRED`.
+- `inventory.errors`: new `ReservationNotFound`, `ReservationExpired`,
+  `InvalidReservationState`, `IdempotencyConflict` (all subclass
+  `InventoryError`); reuse the existing errors where they fit.
+- Time comes only from the injected clock. There is no background job: expiry
+  must take effect without anyone calling a sweep.
+
+The hold lifetime may also be set through an optional keyword argument on the
+`InventoryService` constructor. Its default is 20 minutes.
+
+## Terms
+
+- **Held**: units of a SKU in a warehouse covered by reservations that are
+  ACTIVE and not yet expired.
+- **Expired**: a reservation is expired when the clock time is at or after
+  its `expires_at` (`now >= expires_at`), unless it was confirmed or released
+  before that time.
+- **Requested items**: the `items` argument passed to `reserve`. This is
+  different from `Reservation.items`, which records what was actually held.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+### Availability
+
+1. `available(sku, warehouse)` equals `on_hand − allocated − held`, floored at
+   0.
+2. `allocated(sku, warehouse)` still counts only PLACED orders. Holds are not
+   included.
+3. Held units are unavailable to other reservations and to `place_order`.
+4. When a hold expires, its units become available again at `expires_at`
+   without any further call to the service. Calling `available` right after
+   the clock passes `expires_at` already shows the freed units.
+5. Releasing a hold makes its units available again immediately.
+6. `stock_report` keeps its current `StockRow` shape
+   (`sku, on_hand, allocated, available`). Its `available` value follows
+   criterion 1.
+
+### Reserve
+
+7. Validation matches `place_order`. Each of these rejects the whole request
+   and creates nothing:
+   - an unknown warehouse → `UnknownWarehouse`;
+   - any unknown SKU → `UnknownSku`;
+   - any quantity that is not a positive int (bools included) →
+     `InvalidQuantity`;
+   - empty `items` → `InvalidOrder`.
+
+   This applies even if the other lines are valid.
+8. Holding fills partially, line by line. Each requested line holds
+   `min(requested, available now)` units.
+9. A line that can hold nothing is left out of `Reservation.items`. It does
+   not appear with 0.
+10. If no line can hold anything, `reserve` raises `InsufficientStock` and
+    creates nothing.
+11. Otherwise a new reservation is returned with:
+    - `status == ACTIVE`;
+    - `order_id is None`;
+    - `warehouse` as requested;
+    - `items` as described in criteria 8 and 9;
+    - `expires_at == clock.now() + hold lifetime` (20 minutes by default).
+12. Reservation IDs are sequential: `RES-0001`, `RES-0002`, …
+13. Partial filling applies only to reservations. `place_order` stays all or
+    nothing.
+
+### Idempotency
+
+14. Idempotency keys are unique across the whole service, not per warehouse.
+15. A `reserve` call that reuses a recorded key with the same warehouse and
+    the same requested items behaves like this:
+    - It returns the existing reservation (same `reservation_id`) in its
+      current state (ACTIVE, CONFIRMED, RELEASED or EXPIRED).
+    - It does not hold any more stock or fill again, even if more stock has
+      arrived since.
+    - It does not change `expires_at`.
+
+    Requested items count as the same when they map the same SKUs to the same
+    quantities, in any key order. They are compared against the original
+    request, not against what ended up held.
+16. A valid `reserve` call that reuses a recorded key with a different
+    warehouse or different requested items raises `IdempotencyConflict` and
+    changes nothing.
+17. Input validation (criterion 7) runs before the key check. An invalid
+    request with a recorded key raises the validation error, not
+    `IdempotencyConflict`.
+18. A `reserve` call that fails does not record its key, whether it failed
+    validation or with `InsufficientStock`. A later call with that key is
+    treated as new.
+
+### Confirm
+
+19. Confirming an ACTIVE, unexpired reservation does the following:
+    - It creates a PLACED `Order` in the reservation's warehouse with exactly
+      the held items, and returns that order.
+    - The order ID comes from the normal `ORD-` sequence.
+    - `placed_at` is the clock time.
+    - The reservation becomes CONFIRMED, and its `order_id` is set to the new
+      order's ID.
+20. After confirmation, the held units count as allocated to the order, not
+    as held. `available` does not change at the moment of confirmation.
+21. Confirming an ACTIVE, unexpired hold always succeeds. This holds even if
+    a write-off has since pushed on-hand below the held quantity.
+22. Confirming an already CONFIRMED reservation returns the same order
+    (same `order_id`) and creates nothing new.
+23. Confirming an expired reservation raises `ReservationExpired`. This
+    includes confirming exactly at `expires_at`.
+24. Confirming a RELEASED reservation raises `InvalidReservationState`.
+25. The resulting order behaves like any other order: it can be shipped and
+    cancelled through the existing methods.
+
+### Release
+
+26. Releasing an ACTIVE, unexpired reservation sets its status to RELEASED and
+    returns it.
+27. Releasing an already RELEASED reservation returns it unchanged, with no
+    error.
+28. Releasing an expired reservation returns it unchanged with status
+    EXPIRED, with no error.
+29. Releasing a CONFIRMED reservation raises `InvalidReservationState`. The
+    caller should cancel the order instead.
+
+### Lookup and status
+
+30. `get_reservation` returns the reservation with its current status. A hold
+    past `expires_at` that was never confirmed or released reports EXPIRED,
+    without any sweep having been called.
+31. Every reservation that `get_reservation`, `reserve` or
+    `release_reservation` returns reports its expiry status correctly as of
+    the current clock time.
+32. An unknown `reservation_id` raises `ReservationNotFound` from
+    `get_reservation`, `confirm_reservation` and `release_reservation`.
+
+### General
+
+33. All new errors subclass `InventoryError`.
+34. No package code reads the wall clock. All timestamps and expiry decisions
+    use the injected clock.
+35. The existing test suite keeps passing unchanged.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Partial confirm or partial release of a reservation.
+- Changing a reservation's items after it is created.
+- Extending or refreshing a hold's expiry.
+- A reservation that spans several warehouses.
+- A background sweeper or any scheduled job.
+- A separate "reserved" column in `stock_report`.
+- Persistence beyond the in-memory repository.
+- Expiring or reusing idempotency keys over time.
